### 7. Sudha et al. (2026)

Bio Trace – A Blockchain and IPFS Based Traceability Platform for Secure Ayurvedic Herb Supply Chains

International Journal of Latest Technology in Engineering, Management & Applied Science

Research contributions

- Proposed BioTrace, combining Hyperledger Fabric and IPFS for Ayurvedic herb traceability.
- Introduced role-based access control for supply-chain participants.
- Incorporated geo-fencing and seasonal validation to support cultivation and harvesting verification.
- Included species verification and laboratory quality metrics in the traceability framework.
- Proposed QR-code-based consumer verification.
- Addressed traceability from harvesting to consumer delivery.

Research gaps

- Requires independent evaluation of the accuracy and reliability of species verification.
- Needs validation of geo-fencing and seasonal rules across different medicinal plant species and geographical regions.
- Requires integration with standardized laboratory testing and automated adulteration detection.
- Needs performance evaluation under large-scale, multi-stakeholder supply-chain conditions.
- Requires empirical assessment of data authenticity, sensor reliability, and the accuracy of information submitted by producers.
- The effectiveness of the platform in actual Ayurvedic supply chains needs to be established through field deployment.

