## 2. Consolidated research gaps across all seven papers

Comparing the papers reveals the following major research gaps.

| No. | Identified research gap | Explanation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| G1 | Lack of physical authentication | Blockchain stores records but cannot independently prove that the physical herb matches its digital identity. |
| G2 | Limited AI/ML integration | Existing blockchain systems do not comprehensively integrate machine learning for medicinal plant species identification and adulteration detection. |
| G3 | Lack of chemical quality verification | Laboratory results and spectral fingerprints are not consistently linked to individual blockchain batches. |
| G4 | Incomplete end-to-end traceability | Some approaches emphasize circulation or harvesting without fully connecting cultivation, collection, processing, storage, distribution, and consumption. |
| G5 | Lack of standardized data integration | Different stakeholders use different formats, databases, quality parameters, and identification methods. |
| G6 | Limited real-time monitoring | Environmental conditions such as temperature, humidity, and storage conditions are not consistently monitored throughout transportation. |
| G7 | Data reliability and authenticity | Blockchain ensures that recorded information is difficult to alter, but cannot guarantee that the original information entered is correct. |
| G8 | Limited practical validation | Many proposed approaches lack extensive real-world deployment and quantitative performance evaluation. |
| G9 | Scalability and interoperability | Integration among farmers, collectors, laboratories, manufacturers, distributors, and regulatory authorities remains challenging. |
| G10 | Limited sustainability verification | There is insufficient integration of harvesting permissions, endangered-species protection, sustainable collection, and geographical compliance. |

## 3. Overall research contributions of the existing literature

The seven papers collectively contribute to five important research areas:

1. Blockchain-based traceability

Provides immutable, transparent, and auditable records of medicinal-herb transactions and supply-chain activities.

Booker/Heinrich et al. (2019), Shi et al. (2020), Han et al. (2025)

2. IoT-enabled monitoring

Enables data collection and transmission from supply-chain activities and connected sensing devices.

Shi et al. (2020), Januszek et al. (2021)

3. Physical and chemical authentication

Establishes the need to verify the actual botanical material using spectroscopy, NMR, and laboratory testing.

Januszek et al. (2021), Feng et al. (2025)

4. Pharmaceutical and herbal supply-chain management

Provides frameworks for tracking medicinal products, ensuring accountability, and supporting regulatory compliance.

Akram et al. (2024), Han et al. (2025)

5. Decentralized Ayurvedic traceability

Introduces Hyperledger Fabric, IPFS, geo-fencing, seasonal validation, laboratory metrics, and consumer-facing QR verification.

Sudha et al. (2026)

