## 5. Recommended research framework

A promising research direction is to develop an AI- and IoT-integrated blockchain framework for authentic and quality-assured medicinal herb supply chains.

## Proposed integrated framework

1. Medicinal herb cultivation and collection

Farmer details, species, geographical location, harvesting date and season

2. IoT-based data collection

Temperature, humidity, location and storage conditions

3. AI/ML-based herb authentication

Species identification, classification and adulteration screening

4. Spectroscopy and laboratory quality verification

Chemical fingerprinting, active constituent analysis and quality assessment

5. Blockchain and IPFS integration

Immutable batch records, laboratory report hashes, smart contracts and provenance tracking

6. Consumer and regulatory verification

QR-code verification, product history, quality certificates and compliance information

The important distinction is that AI and laboratory testing verify the physical material, IoT monitors supply-chain conditions, and blockchain maintains the integrity and traceability of the resulting records.

## 6. Suggested research problem statement

The following can be adapted for your research proposal or paper.

Writing

### Research Problem Statement

Existing blockchain-based medicinal herb traceability systems primarily emphasize secure information storage, supply-chain transparency, and transaction tracking. However, blockchain alone cannot guarantee the physical authenticity, botanical identity, and chemical quality of medicinal herbs. Furthermore, existing approaches demonstrate limited integration of artificial intelligence, IoT-based environmental monitoring, spectroscopy-based authentication, and laboratory quality verification within a unified traceability framework.

Consequently, there is a need for an integrated and intelligent traceability framework that combines blockchain, IoT, machine learning, and chemical quality assessment to establish reliable end-to-end provenance and authenticity of medicinal herbs. Such a framework should enable continuous monitoring, automated verification, secure quality-data management, and transparent access to product information for all supply-chain stakeholders.

